ARC Prize 2026: ARC-AGI-2 has been upgraded to L4x4s. Thank you @kaggle for upgrading compute for all participants.

@arcprize: "Also live today: ARC Prize 2026 - 3 tracks, $2,000,000 in prizes available! Get involved: Play a Game, Build Agents, Win Prizes." 

The ARC Prize aims to advance open-source AGI research, offering significant rewards for innovative contributions.

In order to ensure that ARC-AGI-3 games were solvable by humans, we tested over 450 people. If a game was too hard, we either revised it or discarded it completely. For maximum transparency, we have open-sourced all the human replays for public games.

My favorite charts that come out of these are the Action Progression charts, which make it much easier to understand how efficient environment solves translate into scores.

@arcprize: "Today we're open-sourcing the ARC-AGI-3 Human Baseline. This is the most exhaustive human testing study in the ARC-AGI series. Every environment was solved by at least 2 people (many by more) from the general public, with no prior training."

Exactly. The number of iterations you do is far more important than the amount of 'thinking' you do. It doesn't matter how intelligent or knowledgeable you are; reality will always push back. The world is complex, and every problem you solve will lead to ten new problems you will need to solve afterwards. It never ends.

The practical 'AI literacy' advice from this is to ensure you get rich, visual, legible, interactive feedback with every iteration, so that you are actually updating your mental model and not incurring understanding debt. The models will do exactly what you tell them to do, regardless of how smart or dumb it is.

@fchollet: "You cannot think your way to a perfect design. Only building and testing, over many iterations, can reveal the flaws in your mental model and provide the feedback you need to create the best design possible."

